# Design Document

> The desktop app lives in `apps/desktop/`; paths below are relative to it. The public
> landing site (`apps/landing/`) is described in [LANDING.md](LANDING.md).

Controller-first desktop game launcher for macOS, Windows, and Linux (macOS Apple Silicon
first). Orbit is a modern alternative to Pegasus Frontend, inspired by PS5, Nintendo Switch,
and Apple TV.

Built with **Tauri 2**, **React**, **TypeScript**, and **SQLite**. All filesystem and
process logic lives in Rust; the frontend never launches games directly.

## Purpose

- Fullscreen, couch-friendly game library with cinematic screen transitions.
- Import games from Steam, Astris (`.nsp`/`.xci`), GameHub, macOS apps, and manual paths.
- Track playtime, install size, and achievements locally.
- One restrained glass console look with a Rust-generated material texture and portable compositor surfaces. Built-in game themes bundle logos and heroes. Theme Studio searches SteamGridDB logos and offers backgrounds from configured game artwork providers. Retired platform and color presets resolve to Liquid Glass. Custom artwork selection is explicit. Missing artwork is reported by the picker. Existing custom files remain on disk; the interface uses Liquid Glass styling.

## Functional requirements

- User can browse a **home** screen with hero artwork and recent-game rows.
- User can browse, filter, and sort the **library** grid.
- User can open **game details** with playtime, size, achievements, and launch action.
- User can **add games** via importer scans or manual file pick (Add game journey).
- User can **edit properties** — title, description, portrait/landscape/square artwork (cached files or small inline refs in SQLite).
  Each artwork slot offers **Browse images** (a grid of results from SteamGridDB, TheGamesDB, RAWG, Wikimedia and the Steam Store, filterable by source) and **Random SteamGridDB** (applies a random SteamGridDB image for the matched game, avoiding the previous roll). Picks only apply to the draft until Save.
- Every imported game ends up with artwork in all slots: enrichment runs for new games and for existing games still missing real art; anything still empty gets a reused slot or a generated placeholder that stays eligible for later enrichment. Importer titles are corrected from a matched SteamGridDB / TheGamesDB / Steam Store entry (exact normalized title or Steam app ID only).
- User can **launch** a game; Orbit tracks the session and playtime.
- User can **stop** an active game session from the UI.
- User can sync **Steam** library metadata (playtime, SizeOnDisk, achievements) locally.
- User can **enrich** newly imported Steam/GameHub games with store descriptions and SteamGridDB artwork (optional API key); existing games via Edit Properties → Fetch metadata.
- User can switch **themes** including custom JSON themes from Application Support.
- User can navigate entirely with a **gamepad** or keyboard fallback.

## Non-functional requirements

- **Local-first:** SQLite is the source of truth; no cloud sync in v1.
- **Controller-first:** spatial focus system; all primary flows reachable without a mouse.
- **Desktop-first:** macOS Apple Silicon remains the primary release target, with Windows and Linux install paths; fullscreen window management via Tauri.
- **Safe launch:** Rust validates paths and only launches resolved executables from known importers.
- **Performance:** avoid unbounded library loads; batch Tauri invokes where possible.

## Out of scope

ROM downloads/decryption, keys management, emulator distribution, Steam account auth, cloud sync,
social features. Bulk “enrich entire library” jobs and Switch title-ID databases are deferred past 0.2.

